{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#0_1018217201","relevance":2,"reason":"This Stanford article segment is directly about students and parents being unaware that students have been tracked out of college-preparatory math and science classes, but the shown passage does not provide the Latino-specific count or percentage.","learned":"Another Stanford segment says students intending to go to college and their parents often do not know when a student has been tracked out of college-preparatory science and math classes."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#0_1018217201","reason":"Retrieve the full segment because it is from the highly relevant Stanford article and may include nearby Latino/Hispanic-specific context or the exact answer-bearing statistic."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#0_1018217201"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#0_1018217201","document":"SCHOOL TRACKING HARMS MILLIONS, SOCIOLOGIST FINDS ...  SCHOOL TRACKING HARMS MILLIONS, SOCIOLOGIST FINDS ... SCHOOL TRACKING HARMS MILLIONS, SOCIOLOGIST FINDS 03/02/94 CONTACT: Stanford University News Service (650) 723-2558 SCHOOL TRACKING HARMS MILLIONS, SOCIOLOGIST FINDS STANFORD - A new study on tracking in high schools shows the system of  placing some students in college preparatory courses and others in easier  math and science courses is \\\"harming millions of students in American  society,\\\" says Sanford Dornbusch, the Reed-Hodgson Professor of Human  Biology, who holds joint appointments in the Department of Sociology and the  School of Education at Stanford University. Tracking doesn't limit opportunities for the top tenth or so of students  but is particularly disastrous for students whose abilities fall in the  middle range, Dornbusch said. Eighth grade test scores are critical to a students' high school  placement, yet many who do well on those tests - particularly Latino and  African American students in the Northern California schools studied - are  misplaced in courses below their abilities, said Dornbusch, who led a  research team on the subject. The tracking system also misplaced about a fifth of white students who  aspire to go to four-year colleges, he said. \\\"At minimum, more students should be in higher tracks,\\\" Dornbusch said. Perhaps even more shocking, he said, are findings that students who intend  to go to college and their parents often don't know when a student has been  tracked out of college-preparatory science and math classes. Biology courses  with names like \\\"Ecology and You\\\" or \\\"Life on Earth\\\" hide the fact that they  will not prepare a student for admission to a four-year college. Similarly, a  student assigned to business math or other courses that teachers tend to  nickname \\\"bonehead\\\" math will find it hard to get back on track for admission  to a four-year college. School principals and teachers may not want students and parents to know  which courses are college preparation, Dornbusch suggested in his recent  inaugural address as president of the Society for Research on Adolescence.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
