{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#0_1018217201","relevance":2,"reason":"The full Stanford segment is highly related and directly says particularly Latino and African American students are misplaced below their abilities and that students/parents often do not know they have been tracked out of college-preparatory math and science, but it does not give the Latino-specific 'how many' figure in this segment.","learned":"The Stanford article's opening frames the issue as high-school tracking: many capable students, particularly Latino and African American students in Northern California schools, are placed below ability and often do not know they are outside college-prep science/math tracks."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1539","reason":"The title-and-researcher query is retrieving segments of the strongly relevant Stanford article; checking the next result may uncover remaining unjudged segments with exact figures or further direct evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1539"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#1_1018219565","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1539","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#1_1018219565\" , \"end_char\": 3038.000000 , \"headings\": \"<cover>SCHOOL TRACKING HARMS MILLIONS, SOCIOLOGIST FINDS\" , \"segment\": \"\\\"At minimum, more students should be in higher tracks,\\\" Dornbusch </cover>said. Perhaps even more shocking, he said, are findings that students who intend  to go to college and their parents often don't know when a student has been  tracked out of college-preparatory science and math classes. Biology courses  with names like \"Ecology and You\" or \"Life on Earth\" hide the fact that they  will not prepare a student for admission to a four-year college. Similarly, a  student assigned to business math or other courses that teachers tend to  nickname \"bonehead\" math will find it hard to get back on track for admission  to a four-year college. School principals and teachers may not want students and parents to know  which courses are college preparation, Dornbusch suggested in his recent  inaugural address as president of the Society for Research on Adolescence. If  they knew, more students and parents would complain and demand better  placements, "}}
