{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#1_1018219565","relevance":2,"reason":"This Stanford article segment is directly about students and parents not knowing when students have been tracked out of college-preparatory science and math classes, but the shown snippet does not include the Latino-specific count or percentage.","learned":"The segment reinforces the education-misinformation mechanism: course names and tracking practices can hide from students and parents that classes are not college-preparatory."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#1_1018219565","reason":"Retrieve the full segment because it is from the strongly relevant Stanford article and may include the continuation leading into Latino-specific placement statistics."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#1_1018219565"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#1_1018219565","document":"SCHOOL TRACKING HARMS MILLIONS, SOCIOLOGIST FINDS ...  SCHOOL TRACKING HARMS MILLIONS, SOCIOLOGIST FINDS ... \\\"At minimum, more students should be in higher tracks,\\\" Dornbusch said. Perhaps even more shocking, he said, are findings that students who intend  to go to college and their parents often don't know when a student has been  tracked out of college-preparatory science and math classes. Biology courses  with names like \\\"Ecology and You\\\" or \\\"Life on Earth\\\" hide the fact that they  will not prepare a student for admission to a four-year college. Similarly, a  student assigned to business math or other courses that teachers tend to  nickname \\\"bonehead\\\" math will find it hard to get back on track for admission  to a four-year college. School principals and teachers may not want students and parents to know  which courses are college preparation, Dornbusch suggested in his recent  inaugural address as president of the Society for Research on Adolescence. If  they knew, more students and parents would complain and demand better  placements, he said. Dornbusch urged researchers to consider tracking to be a \\\"key variable\\\" in  their studies of youth and what leads to success, but he also said enough  studies already indict tracking, so that school policymakers should consider  changing their practices now. Jeannie Oakes of the University of California-Los Angeles concluded that  \\\"the tracking system is more a product of inertia in a school system than of  the abilities of students\\\" in the schools she studied, Dornbusch said. The Stanford tracking study led by Dornbusch involved analyzing all school  records since the fifth grade for approximately 1,200 students in six diverse  San Francisco Bay Area high schools. The researchers selected at random an  equal proportion of students with high, middle and low grades for each  gender-ethnicity combination so that there were no ethnic differences in  school grades for their sample.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"15/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#1_1018219565","relevance":2,"reason":"The full Stanford segment is directly related to educational misinformation because it says college-aspiring students and parents often do not know students have been tracked out of college-preparatory math/science, and it introduces the 1,200-student Stanford tracking study, but this segment does not give the Latino-specific 'how many' answer.","learned":"The Stanford study analyzed records for about 1,200 students in six diverse San Francisco Bay Area high schools, sampled equally by gender-ethnicity and grades, and found tracking can be hidden from students and parents."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1539","reason":"The title-and-researcher query is yielding consecutive Stanford article segments; continuing may reveal any remaining unjudged segments with direct Latino/Hispanic figures or supporting evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1539"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#10_1018237027","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1539","rank":8,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#10_1018237027\" , \"end_char\": 9579.000000 , \"headings\": \"<cover>SCHOOL TRACKING HARMS MILLIONS, SOCIOLOGIST FINDS\" , \"segment\": \"\\\"High school placements made in the eighth grade have profound  occupational and educational outcomes,\\\" Dornbusch </cover>concluded. \" For students in  the middle, these decisions are more arbitrary and less likely to be based on  ability.\" Funding for the study was provided by the Carnegie Foundation of New York,  the Spencer Foundation, the Drown Foundation of Los Angeles and Jill and Boyd  Smith. The research team included Brad Brown of the University of Wisconsin,  Larry Steinberg of Temple University, and Zen-yin Chen, Phil Ritter, Harriet  Romo, Ricardo Stanton-Salazar and Ken Wood of Stanford. -kpo/tracking- 940302Arc4396.html This is an archived release. This release is not available in any other form. Images mentioned in this release are not available online. Stanford News Service has an extensive library of images, some of which may be available to you online. Direct your request by EMail to newslibrary@stanford.edu.\" , \"start_char\": 8647.","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#6_1018229726","rank":4,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#4_1018225918","rank":5,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#3_1018223792","rank":6,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#2_1018221721","rank":7,"relevance":3}]}}
