{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#10_1018237027","relevance":2,"reason":"This is a concluding segment from the strongly relevant Stanford school-tracking article and supports the education issue by saying eighth-grade high-school placements have profound educational outcomes and can be arbitrary for middle students, but it does not provide the Latino-specific count or misinformation statistic.","learned":"The Stanford article concludes that eighth-grade high-school tracking placements strongly affect later educational and occupational outcomes, and lists the research team and funders."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1539","reason":"The title-and-researcher query has produced multiple relevant Stanford article segments; continue once more to see whether any remaining unjudged segment contains direct Latino/Hispanic statistics or supporting evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1539"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#9_1018235265","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1539","rank":9,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#9_1018235265\" , \"end_char\": 9309.000000 , \"headings\": \"<cover>SCHOOL TRACKING HARMS MILLIONS, SOCIOLOGIST FINDS\" , \"segment\": \"They did another analysis just of those students whose eighth grade math  scores put them in the top half. All such students, in their view, should  have been assigned to college preparatory math. \\\"The proportion of these students mis-assigned to lower track classes  without their knowledge was about 30 percent for the disadvantaged minorities  (African Americans and Hispanics) and about 13 percent for the others,\\\"  Dornbusch </cover>said. They also found the original placement in science for these students  strongly affected the probability that they would take chemistry or physics  in their junior and senior years. \" Students of average ability assigned to  [college-prep] biology were 10 times more likely to take chemistry or physics  than students assigned to 'baby biology,' \" he said. \"High school placements made in the eighth grade have profound  occupational and educational outcomes,\" Dornbusch concluded. \" For students in  the middle, these decisions are more arbitrary and less likely to be based on  "}}
