{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ latino latinos hispanic hispanics) \"without their knowledge\" (+ education school classes))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1871306752#5_3455733573","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1544","rank":3,"snippet":"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1871306752#5_3455733573\" , \"end_char\": 5777.000000 , \"headings\": \"Tuskegee Tuskegee Early History Demographics Employment<cover>Education Transportation Events and Places of Interest \" , \"segment\": \"More than 1,000 pilots were trained at historic Moton Field and Tuskegee Army Air Field. In more recent history, the town garnered much negative press as the site of the infamous Tuskegee Syphilis Study, in which African American men were used as subjects, without their knowledge or consent, in a public health study on the effects of untreated syphilis. The experiment resulted in the creation of the National Center for Bioethics in Research and Health Care at Tuskegee University in 1997. Demographics According to 2016 Census estimates, Tuskegee recorded a population of 9,050. Of that number, 95.7 percent identified themselves as African American, 2.6 percent as white, 2.0 percent Hispanic, </cover>1.0 percent as two or more races, 0.5 percent Asian, and 0.1 percent Native American. The median household income was $27,500 and a per capita income is $15,086. Employment According to ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#9_1018235265","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#8_1018233542","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
