{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_29_749326213#7_1712647318","relevance":0,"reason":"The passage is a TechCrunch-style list of technology/business headlines with a generic mention of misinformation; it does not discuss Latinos/Hispanics or misinformation about education or college.","learned":"Broad 'Latino/Hispanic misinformation college' wording can retrieve irrelevant technology-news navigation where misinformation is unrelated to education."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#9_1018235265","reason":"Retrieve the full Stanford segment containing the strongest direct quantified evidence—about 30% of African American and Hispanic students mis-assigned to lower-track classes without their knowledge—to confirm complete grade-3 support."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#9_1018235265"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_580603816#9_1018235265","document":"SCHOOL TRACKING HARMS MILLIONS, SOCIOLOGIST FINDS ...  SCHOOL TRACKING HARMS MILLIONS, SOCIOLOGIST FINDS ... They did another analysis just of those students whose eighth grade math  scores put them in the top half. All such students, in their view, should  have been assigned to college preparatory math. \\\"The proportion of these students mis-assigned to lower track classes  without their knowledge was about 30 percent for the disadvantaged minorities  (African Americans and Hispanics) and about 13 percent for the others,\\\"  Dornbusch said. They also found the original placement in science for these students  strongly affected the probability that they would take chemistry or physics  in their junior and senior years. \\\" Students of average ability assigned to  [college-prep] biology were 10 times more likely to take chemistry or physics  than students assigned to 'baby biology,' \\\" he said. \\\"High school placements made in the eighth grade have profound  occupational and educational outcomes,\\\" Dornbusch concluded. \\\" For students in  the middle, these decisions are more arbitrary and less likely to be based on  ability.\\\" Funding for the study was provided by the Carnegie Foundation of New York,  the Spencer Foundation, the Drown Foundation of Los Angeles and Jill and Boyd  Smith. The research team included Brad Brown of the University of Wisconsin,  Larry Steinberg of Temple University, and Zen-yin Chen, Phil Ritter, Harriet  Romo, Ricardo Stanton-Salazar and Ken Wood of Stanford. -kpo/tracking- 940302Arc4396.html This is an archived release.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
